// TreeLock — ONE ADVISORY LOCK PER CHECKOUT, held by the commands that write the checkout's SHARED
// build products, so two of them can never run in one tree at once.
//
// ⭐ **THE RULE THIS REPLACES WAS PROSE, AND PROSE IS NOT A GATE.** `.claude/CLAUDE.md` and the rung
// skill both carried a sentence — *"NEVER run two suites in one tree at once"* — that a second agent
// cannot see and a timed-out MCP call cannot honour. Two races were MEASURED against it in one
// session:
//
//   1. **Two suites → `specs/.spec-tmp` → a FALSE RED.** Both runs stage their fragments under
//      the same per-spec scratch directory, so one deletes what the other is compiling and a lane
//      that is green reports `FAIL exit 1`.
//   2. **Two builds → `maxon-bin/.maxon/` → a FALSE SLOW, then a silent `exit 1`.** A 12-minute
//      build printing nothing but `error-codes: OK` — and it made a reviewer report the change under
//      review as a compile-time regression. **A race that manifests as SLOW is worse than one that
//      manifests as WRONG, because slow gets attributed to the change under test.**
//
// The project had already reasoned its way to the cure one level up and stopped there: the slice
// board's *"`git push` IS THE LOCK"* is a real, contended, machine-checked lock for the BOARD. This
// is the same thing for the TREE.
//
// ============================================================================================
// WHAT IT LOCKS, AND WHO TAKES IT
// ============================================================================================
//
// **ONE lock per CHECKOUT, covering `build` AND the two harnesses together.** Not per output
// directory — a suite RUNS the binary a build REPLACES, so the two contend even though they write
// different directories, which is exactly the pairing `E6002`'s message already names as its likeliest
// cause. Not per machine either: every worktree carries its own `stdlib/`, `runtime/`, `bin/` and
// `.maxon/`, so worktrees must never block each other (that is the whole point of working in one).
//
// **The checkout is the one CONTAINING THE PATH THE COMMAND WRITES INTO** — the project directory, the
// spec directory, the scale corpus root — found by the same upward walk for `stdlib/` that locates the
// compiler's own source (`stdlibDirAbove`). A path with no checkout above it has nothing shared to
// guard, and takes no lock: `maxon build /tmp/scratch.maxon` is not a tree-level command.
//
// ⚠ **`stdlib/` ALONE IDENTIFIES THE CHECKOUT, AND ONE LOCK COVERS EVERY TIER IN IT.** `runtime/` is
// that directory's sibling under the same root, so the walk already lands on the tree that holds both;
// a second marker would only re-derive the same answer, and a second lock would split a guard whose
// whole point is that ONE command at a time writes the tree.
//
// Three commands take it (`Main`): `build <directory>` with no `--output=`, `spec-test`, `scale-test`.
// What they have in common is that they write the tree's own output directories; everything else — a
// one-off file build, an `--output=` build, `fmt`, `error-codes` — writes where the CALLER said and
// takes nothing. That distinction is what keeps the suite's thousands of child `build … --output=…`
// compiles free of it, and it is the ⛔ that would otherwise be a deadlock rather than a lock.
//
// `tests/spec-harness` runs `spec-test` at fixtures it copies under the host temp directory,
// outside any checkout, so those runs take nothing and never contend with each other or with a
// developer's own suite.
//
// ============================================================================================
// LIVENESS IS A HEARTBEAT, NOT A PID PROBE. THIS IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE.
// ============================================================================================
//
// A stale lock that outlives its holder blocks every later command forever, and a holder can die
// without releasing it: killed by hand, or ended with its process tree when an MCP tool call
// reaches its timeout.
//
// So the record is REWRITTEN while its holder works (`touchTreeLock`, throttled to one write every
// `TouchIntervalMs`), and its FILE MTIME is the liveness signal: a lock nothing has touched for
// `AbandonedAfterSeconds` is broken automatically, with a printed line saying so.
//
// ⚠ A pid probe answers the wrong question here. The pid is recorded, because a reader needs it to
// end the holder, but it is never tested: a pid is reused, so "alive" can be a stranger, and a
// wedged `maxon` process is alive forever while making no progress. The heartbeat asks whether the
// holder is still getting anywhere, which is right for both.
//
// ⚠ **THE MTIME IS A WALL CLOCK, AND A WALL CLOCK CAN STEP BACKWARDS** (NTP, a timezone change, a VM
// resuming from a snapshot — see `WallClock`'s header). That is forced, not chosen: two processes
// share no monotonic origin, so a timestamp one writes and another reads can only be wall time. A
// backwards step is clamped to "no time has passed", which errs toward keeping a live lock rather than
// breaking it.
//
// ============================================================================================
// THE RECORD IS ECHOED, NOT RE-DERIVED
// ============================================================================================
//
// A refusal QUOTES the record it found rather than reformatting parsed fields, so a field a reader
// does not know is still displayed, and the only thing a reader must parse is the one key it acts on.
// The lines are plain `key=value` and may not be reordered for prettiness.

// The three constants below are the record's shared terms: the file name, the heartbeat and the
// abandonment window. Every reader takes them from here rather than spelling its own, so two
// commands cannot disagree about which lock they are contending for or when one is dead.

// Where the record lives, relative to the checkout root: at the top, visibly, under a name that says
// what it is. Gitignored (`/.maxon-tree.lock`).
let LockFileName = ".maxon-tree.lock"

// The record's fields. `heldSeconds` is computed BY THE HOLDER — which knows when it started — so a
// reader needs no clock arithmetic and no decimal parser to say how long this has been going on.
let PidField = "pid"
let TokenField = "token"
let SinceField = "sinceUnix"
let HeldField = "heldSeconds"
let ArgvField = "argv"
let FieldSeparator = "="
let RecordLineSeparator = "\n"
let ArgvWordSeparator = " "

// How often the holder rewrites the record, and how long without one before it is treated as
// abandoned. The gap between them is the whole margin: the interval must be far smaller than the
// window so ordinary jitter — a slow phase, a busy disk — never reads as a dead holder, and the window
// must be small enough that a KILLED holder does not wedge the tree for long. 5 s against 60 s is a
// factor of twelve, and both directions of being wrong are cheap and visible: a break that should not
// have happened prints a line saying it broke a lock, and a wait that should not have happened is
// bounded by one minute.
let TouchIntervalMs = 5000
let AbandonedAfterSeconds = 60

// The pause between writing the record and reading it back — see `takeLock`.
let AcquireVerifyDelayMs = 20

// Whole seconds a lock has been held, or has gone untouched. A duration, not an instant: it is what
// the refusal prints and what the abandonment window compares against.
typealias LockSeconds = int(0 to u64.max)

// The lock this process wrote. A run that found no checkout above its operand owns nothing: there
// is nothing for it to keep alive and nothing for it to remove.
type OwnedLock
	export let path as FilePath
	export let token as String
	export let sinceUnix as UnixSeconds

	static function create(path FilePath, token String, sinceUnix UnixSeconds) returns OwnedLock
		return Self{path: path, token: token, sinceUnix: sinceUnix}
	end 'create'
end 'OwnedLock'

typealias OwnedLockArray = Array with OwnedLock

// ⚠ **AT MOST ONE ELEMENT, AND THE ARRAY IS THE OPTIONAL** — the shape a module-level global can
// actually hold. A global's initializer must be a CONSTANT EXPRESSION (E2045), so no struct- or
// union-valued global can be constructed at module scope; an empty `Array.create()` is one, and from
// then on the state is MUTATED in place rather than reassigned. That is the same shape
// `PhaseProbe.openPhaseSpans` uses for the same reason, and it is why the emptiness test below reads
// as a count.
//
// ⚠ It also steers clear of a compiler defect: assigning a union value to a global stores the WRONG
// SSA value (`std.global_store_i64 @g %1` picks up `mm_alloc`'s size argument instead of the box),
// which is a silent wrong answer on both x64 and arm64 and an `E9001` crash whenever the stale id has
// no home in the storing function. Do not "simplify" this into a `union` global until that is fixed.
var ownedLocks = OwnedLockArray.create()

// When the record was last rewritten, on the MONOTONIC clock — the right instrument for "how long
// since", as opposed to the wall clock stamped INTO the record, which is the right one for "when".
//
// It starts at zero rather than at `Clock.nowMs()`, which is both what a module-level initializer can
// express and the right value anyway: a process holding no lock never reads it, and the first touch
// after one is taken rewrites a record `takeLock` has just written.
var lastTouchMs = 0 as InstantMs

// What one read of the lock file found. `present` carries the record's own bytes (echoed verbatim by
// the refusal) and how long ago it was last touched (the liveness signal, read from the mtime rather
// than from anything inside it).
union LockRecord
	absent
	present(text String, untouchedSeconds LockSeconds)
end 'LockRecord'

// Stopping answers `NothingRanExitCode`, the code `refuseIfStale` answers, because both mean the
// same thing to a caller reading only the number: nothing ran.
//
// A refusal is immediate and never a wait: a silent wait turns a fast, legible error into a hang
// indistinguishable from a wedge.
export function acquireTreeLock(operand FilePath) returns ExitCode
	let lockPath = try lockPathFor(operand) otherwise 'noCheckout'
		// Nothing shared to guard. `operand` has no `stdlib/` above it, so no other command in any
		// tree writes what this one is about to write.
		return SucceededExit
	end 'noCheckout'

	return match readLockRecord(lockPath) 'existing'
		absent gives takeLock(lockPath)
		present(text, untouchedSeconds) gives claimOverExisting(lockPath, record: text, untouchedSeconds: untouchedSeconds)
	end 'existing'
end 'acquireTreeLock'

// ⭐ Say the holder is still working. Cheap enough to call from anywhere that runs regularly — the
// write is throttled to one every `TouchIntervalMs`.
//
// It is called from the places that are BOTH periodic and load-bearing: every compile phase
// (`recordPhaseDelta`), every turn of the spec pool's dispatch loop, every scale rung. Those are the
// sites at which "this process is still getting somewhere" is genuinely true, which is what the record
// claims.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNERSHIP TEST COMES FIRST, AND THAT ORDER IS THE COST.** Almost every caller of this holds
// NO lock — every one of the suite's thousands of child compiles reaches `recordPhaseDelta` — so the
// common path must be the cheap one: a lookup on an empty array, and no clock read at all. Measured:
// `scale-test` reports allocations IDENTICAL to the digit at all six rungs against the row logged
// before this existed, so the unlocked path allocates nothing.
export function touchTreeLock()
	let lock = try ownedLocks.get(0) otherwise return

	if Clock.elapsedMs(lastTouchMs) < TouchIntervalMs 'tooSoon'
		return
	end 'tooSoon'

	refreshRecord(lock)
end 'touchTreeLock'

// Give the lock up. A no-op for a process that never took one, so every command may call it on the way
// out without asking whether it holds anything.
//
// ⚠ **IT REMOVES THE FILE ONLY IF THE FILE IS STILL MINE.** A holder that stalled long enough to be
// declared abandoned has had its lock broken and RETAKEN by somebody else; deleting the path blindly
// would then delete a stranger's live lock and hand the tree to a third process. The token is what
// distinguishes them, and it is the reason the record carries one at all.
export function releaseTreeLock()
	let held = try ownedLocks.get(0) otherwise return

	// CLONED out of the array, because `removeOwnRecord` empties the array the borrow would otherwise
	// pin (E3070) — the same reason the spec pool clones a job out of its in-flight slot.
	let owned = held.clone()
	removeOwnRecord(owned)
end 'releaseTreeLock'

// The lock file for the checkout containing `operand`, or a throw when there is no checkout above it.
//
// `operand` is RESOLVED against the working directory first: `scale-test`'s corpus root is the
// relative `.scale-tmp` and a `build maxon-bin` names its project relatively too, and a relative
// single-component path has no parent to walk up from — so an unresolved one would silently answer
// "not in a checkout" for the very commands this lock exists for.
function lockPathFor(operand FilePath) returns FilePath throws CompileError
	let absolute = operand.resolve(Directory.currentPath())
	let stdlibDir = try stdlibDirAbove(absolute)
	let checkoutRoot = try stdlibDir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	let name = try FilePath.from(LockFileName) otherwise panic("TreeLock: '{LockFileName}' is not a valid path component")
	return checkoutRoot.join(name)
end 'lockPathFor'

// Read the record, and how long ago it was last touched.
//
// The mtime is read AFTER the text and a failure to read it is reported as `absent` — that pairing is
// a race with another process breaking or releasing the lock between the two calls, and "it went away"
// is the honest reading of it.
function readLockRecord(lockPath FilePath) returns LockRecord
	let text = try File.readText(lockPath) otherwise return LockRecord.absent
	let info = try File.info(lockPath) otherwise return LockRecord.absent
	return LockRecord.present(text, untouchedSeconds: secondsSince(info.modifiedTime as UnixSeconds))
end 'readLockRecord'

// A record is already there: refuse to the holder, or break it if it has stopped moving.
function claimOverExisting(lockPath FilePath, record String, untouchedSeconds LockSeconds) returns ExitCode
	if untouchedSeconds <= AbandonedAfterSeconds 'stillMoving'
		return reportBusy(lockPath, record: record, untouchedSeconds: untouchedSeconds)
	end 'stillMoving'

	// ⭐ **BREAKING A LOCK IS EXPLICIT AND LOGGED, NEVER SILENT** — the slice board's own rule for a
	// stale claim, and for its reason: if this ever breaks a lock whose holder was merely slow, the
	// line below is the only evidence that would explain the two runs that followed.
	printError("warning: breaking an ABANDONED tree lock at {lockPath.toString()} — nothing has touched it for {untouchedSeconds} s (a live holder rewrites it every {TouchIntervalMs} ms), so the process that took it is gone or has stopped making progress. It said:\n{indentedRecord(record)}\n")
	return takeLock(lockPath)
end 'claimOverExisting'

// ⚠ The read-back is the mutual exclusion, and the pause is what makes it work. Neither the stdlib
// nor the emitted runtime offers an exclusive create (`O_EXCL`), an `flock`, or a `rename` that
// fails on an existing target, so two processes that both find the path free both write it. After
// the pause both read the later writer's bytes, so exactly one survives.
//
// A process that stalls longer than `AcquireVerifyDelayMs` between finding the path free and
// writing it can still overwrite a lock the winner has verified. The contention guarded is
// human-scale, and the failure left is two runs, never a worse one.
function takeLock(lockPath FilePath) returns ExitCode
	let sinceUnix = WallClock.nowUnixSeconds()
	let pid = __Builtins.currentProcessId()

	// Unique across every process that could contend: a pid cannot be shared by two live processes,
	// and the two clocks separate a REUSED pid from the one that held this lock before it.
	let token = "{pid}-{sinceUnix}-{Clock.nowNanos()}"

	// The remedy is in the message because the likeliest reader owns neither the directory nor the
	// problem: an installed Maxon can live somewhere a user cannot write, and `stdlibDirAbove` finds
	// that install's `stdlib/` for any file placed beneath it, so building a `hello.maxon` there lands
	// here. The message says "directory", not "checkout": that reader has no checkout.
	if not writeRecord(lockPath, token: token, sinceUnix: sinceUnix, heldSeconds: 0) 'unwritable'
		printError("error: could not write the tree lock at {lockPath.toString()} — that directory is not writable by you. The lock guards the shared output directories two commands would otherwise corrupt, so nothing was run.\n")
		printError("  If you are building inside an installed copy of Maxon, build from a directory you own instead.\n")
		return NothingRanExitCode
	end 'unwritable'

	sleep(AcquireVerifyDelayMs)

	if not recordCarriesToken(lockPath, token: token) 'lostTheRace'
		printError("error: another maxon command claimed this checkout's tree lock at the same instant, and it won. Nothing was run — re-run once it has finished.\n")
		return NothingRanExitCode
	end 'lostTheRace'

	ownedLocks.push(OwnedLock.create(lockPath, token: token, sinceUnix: sinceUnix))
	lastTouchMs = Clock.nowMs()
	return SucceededExit
end 'takeLock'

// Rewrite the record with a fresh mtime and a fresh `heldSeconds`. A failed write is IGNORED: the tree
// lock is an advisory guard against two developers' commands, and failing a twelve-minute build over
// one unwritable heartbeat would cost far more than the race it prevents. The consequence of a
// swallowed failure is bounded and self-correcting — the lock ages out and the next command breaks it,
// loudly.
function refreshRecord(lock OwnedLock)
	_ = writeRecord(lock.path, token: lock.token, sinceUnix: lock.sinceUnix, heldSeconds: secondsSince(lock.sinceUnix))
	lastTouchMs = Clock.nowMs()
end 'refreshRecord'

// Remove the record, but only while it still carries this process's token — see `releaseTreeLock`.
function removeOwnRecord(lock OwnedLock)
	if not recordCarriesToken(lock.path, token: lock.token) 'brokenAndRetaken'
		return
	end 'brokenAndRetaken'

	try File.delete(lock.path) otherwise ignore
	ownedLocks.clear()
end 'removeOwnRecord'

// The record's bytes. `argv` is the whole command line, because "which of my four terminals is this?"
// is the question a reader actually has, and a bare command name does not answer it.
function writeRecord(lockPath FilePath, token String, sinceUnix UnixSeconds, heldSeconds LockSeconds) returns bool
	let pid = __Builtins.currentProcessId()
	var text = StringBuilder.create()
	appendField(text, key: PidField, value: "{pid}")
	appendField(text, key: TokenField, value: token)
	appendField(text, key: SinceField, value: "{sinceUnix}")
	appendField(text, key: HeldField, value: "{heldSeconds}")
	appendField(text, key: ArgvField, value: joinArgs())

	try File.writeText(lockPath, content: text.build()) otherwise return false
	return true
end 'writeRecord'

function appendField(text StringBuilder, key String, value String)
	text.append("{key}{FieldSeparator}{value}{RecordLineSeparator}")
end 'appendField'

// This process's command line as one line. Newlines in an argument would forge a record field, so they
// are flattened to spaces — an argument holding one is not a thing this compiler's CLI accepts, and a
// record whose fields can be forged by an argument is not a record.
function joinArgs() returns String
	var words = StringArray.create()

	for arg in CommandLine.args() 'each'
		words.push(arg.replace(RecordLineSeparator, with: ArgvWordSeparator))
	end 'each'

	return joinedWith(words, separator: ArgvWordSeparator)
end 'joinArgs'

// Does the record at `lockPath` still name `token` as its holder? The token is the ONE field parsed
// out of a record — everything else is echoed.
function recordCarriesToken(lockPath FilePath, token String) returns bool
	let text = try File.readText(lockPath) otherwise return false
	return fieldValue(text, key: TokenField) == token
end 'recordCarriesToken'

// The value of one `key=` line, or the empty String when the record does not carry that key. Empty is
// a real answer here rather than a sentinel: the only caller compares it against a token that is never
// empty, so a truncated or foreign record answers "not yours", which is the correct reading of it.
//
// `replaceFirst` off a line already known to START with the key, rather than `split("=")` and part 1 —
// which is the text between the FIRST and SECOND `=`, so any value containing one is silently
// truncated. `argv` routinely contains several (`--filter=…`, `--compiler=…`), and
// `CommandLine.optionValue` carries the scar from making exactly that mistake.
function fieldValue(text String, key String) returns String
	let prefix = "{key}{FieldSeparator}"

	for line in text.split(RecordLineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		if line.startsWith(prefix) 'found'
			return line.replaceFirst(prefix, with: "")
		end 'found'
	end 'eachLine'

	return ""
end 'fieldValue'

// Whole seconds from a past wall-clock stamp to now, clamped at zero — see this file's header on why a
// wall clock is forced here and what it does when it steps backwards.
function secondsSince(stamp UnixSeconds) returns LockSeconds
	let now = WallClock.nowUnixSeconds()

	if now <= stamp 'clockWentBackwards'
		return 0
	end 'clockWentBackwards'

	return now - stamp
end 'secondsSince'

// ⭐ The refusal. It names the holder, says how long it has been going and when it last moved, quotes
// the record whole, and says what to do about it — the four things a reader needs and the four a bare
// "locked" sends them off to re-derive. `refuseIfStale` is the model: a refusal that says only that it
// refused is a second problem, not a diagnosis.
function reportBusy(lockPath FilePath, record String, untouchedSeconds LockSeconds) returns ExitCode
	printError("error: this checkout is BUSY — another maxon command holds its tree lock, and two of them in one tree corrupt each other's output directories. Nothing was run.\n")
	printError("  lock:  {lockPath.toString()}\n")
	printError("  held by:\n{indentedRecord(record)}\n")
	printError("  last progress: {untouchedSeconds} s ago (a live holder rewrites the record every {TouchIntervalMs} ms)\n")
	printError("  is it still alive? {livenessProbeLine(fieldValue(record, key: PidField))}\n")
	printError("  Wait for it to finish, or end that process. A holder that stops making progress for {AbandonedAfterSeconds} s is treated as abandoned and its lock is broken automatically, with a line saying so.\n")
	return NothingRanExitCode
end 'reportBusy'

// The probe is PRINTED for the reader to run, never performed here: this lock's liveness is the record's
// mtime heartbeat, and the section at the top of this file argues why testing a pid is the wrong rule. What
// the reader needs is the one command their own host answers, spelled exactly.
function livenessProbeLine(pid String) returns String
	if pid.isEmpty() 'recordCarriesNoPid'
		return "the record above carries no {PidField} field, so there is no process to probe — wait for the abandonment window instead"
	end 'recordCarriesNoPid'

	match detectHostTarget().os 'host'
		windows then return "run `tasklist /FI \"PID eq {pid}\"` — it lists the process, or prints that no task matches"
		linux or
			macos then return "run `ps -p {pid}` — it lists the process, or exits 1 with no row"
		wasi then panic("livenessProbeLine: no wasi process hosts this compiler, so none of them can hold a tree lock")
	end 'host'
end 'livenessProbeLine'

// The record, indented under the line that introduces it. Its own trailing newline is dropped so the
// caller's `\n` is the only one — a record ends with one, and two would put a blank line inside the
// message.
function indentedRecord(record String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()

	for line in record.split(RecordLineSeparator) 'eachLine'
		if line.isEmpty() 'blank'
			continue
		end 'blank'

		out.append("    {line}{RecordLineSeparator}")
	end 'eachLine'

	return out.build().trimEnd()
end 'indentedRecord'
